\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{When invariant measurements separate orbits}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Choose a family of gauge-invariant measurements with values in
sets. Every gauge-invariant observable with values in any set
factors uniquely through the attained measurement tuples if and
only if the measurements separate the
residual orbits of \ref{fdip-0112}. Separation means that any two
distinct residual orbits have different tuples.}

\proof{
\p{The measurement tuple assigns a value to each residual orbit.
If it is injective, each attained tuple determines that orbit.
Assign to the tuple the value of the observable on its orbit;
this defines the required map and prescribes each of its values.
Conversely the map sending a joint configuration to its residual
orbit is itself an invariant set-valued observable. If it factors
through the measurements, equal tuples determine equal orbits.}

\p{It also suffices to test all #{k}-valued observables when
#{1\ne0} in #{k}, since the indicators of \ref{fdip-011E}
distinguish orbits. Over the zero ring every such observable is
constant. In either case the set-valued criterion uses only
evaluation on attained tuples. For example, let an integer
measurement have values zero and two on a two-point set. It
separates the points. Every integer polynomial takes congruent
values modulo two at these two inputs, so no such polynomial
gives an indicator of a single point.}
}
